## Langfuse warns that an empty observation page may still have more results

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/changelog/2026-11-03-observations-api-continuation
Published: 2026-11-03
Channel: news

Langfuse is an AI observability platform whose Observations API retrieves records of application operations. Its reliability update lets expensive queries continue across several requests instead of requiring all progress within one request. That changes an assumption that some client pagination loops may make: an empty response page does not necessarily mean the query is finished.

The client first requests observations with the desired filters and page limit. It processes whatever records arrive, then reads meta.cursor from the response. If that cursor is present, the client sends another request using it, even if the previous page contained no observations. The loop finishes only when the cursor is absent. A short page is also insufficient evidence that all results have been returned.

Langfuse says some documentation examples incorrectly stopped when a page was empty and asks users who copied them to update their loops. The corrected examples cover migration, evaluation pipelines, dataset preparation, analytics and trace deletion. The announcement addresses a small share of queries that time out; it does not claim a measured speed improvement for every query.

Why it matters: For teams exporting or evaluating Langfuse records: audit pagination loops for an empty-page exit condition that can silently truncate results.

