## Braintrust argues for keeping agent history searchable and reusable in evaluations

Source: Braintrust — https://www.braintrust.dev/blog/trace-lake
Published: 2026-11-03
Channel: news

Braintrust is an AI evaluation and observability platform. Its Trace Lake article argues that agent execution records need to support more than opening a single slow request. Teams also need to search past decisions, outputs and tool interactions when a new failure pattern becomes apparent. Discarding those records removes evidence that a later investigation may need.

The proposed approach first makes incoming traces quickly searchable through full-text queries and SQL. Next, object storage keeps growing execution history without requiring every query server to retain a complete local copy. The article also identifies keeping sensitive records inside a customer’s cloud as a requirement for some teams. Braintrust presents its Brainstore database as an implementation for these workloads.

Finally, useful production examples should become evaluation cases with enough context to reproduce the behavior. That context may include application state or the resources used by an agent. Continuous analysis then looks for recurring issues across incoming traces. These are architectural requirements and vendor descriptions, rather than a new independent benchmark proving that every deployment meets the article’s performance targets.

Why it matters: For observability teams: evaluate retention, search and reproducibility together before treating a trace store as a source of regression tests.

