## Elastic tests whether its investigation agent checks evidence before grouping alerts

Source: Elastic Observability Labs — https://www.elastic.co/observability-labs/blog/automated-root-cause-analysis-significant-events
Published: 2026-11-03
Channel: news

Elastic Significant Events uses an investigation agent to check log evidence and group related alerts. Its walkthrough follows two rules detecting the same payment failure. The operational problem is that two firing rules can produce duplicate noise without establishing whether their shared explanation is supported by current logs.

First, code detects changes in rule behavior and selects which detections reach the agent. The agent then reruns each rule’s original query, which it cannot rewrite to fit a hypothesis. It measures the returned evidence and groups signals that describe one incident. Code checks the proposed alert before storage and derives its lifecycle state, while the model judges the explanation and severity.

The evaluation suite replays failure scenarios and checks grouping, affected entities, severity, status and required tool calls. Further checks examine whether successive cycles keep one incident and stable topology; model-based judges review the narrative and calibration. The reported run covers seven scenarios without a variance estimate. Elastic explicitly treats it as a smoke test rather than a benchmark, and warns that the evaluated model may differ from the production configuration.

Why it matters: For teams evaluating investigation agents: test required evidence-gathering actions and repeated incident updates alongside the final explanation.

